[{:seon.task/name "report-beyond-its-pulls"
  :seon.task/plan "`seon.problems/report` (`my.root/problems`) is linear in recorded problems and flat in clean tasks, as designed, but each problem set (one signature, one red run, one dead run) costs ~570 KB and ~550 us against ~400 KB and ~330 us priced from its pulls and EDN reads (`seon.cost/units` :db/pull, :db/pull-attr, :edn/read-char; Jolt 00a3b321). Either the read does work beyond its pulls (find it with seon.profile: the :fns of one report call, and `seon.cost/measure` per step) or the estimate misses a term (then derive it and say what work it is). Done when seon.problems-test/problems-reads-only-the-problems and problems-cost-nothing-for-a-clean-program are green with their :overrun keys deleted."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.problems-test/problems-reads-only-the-problems seon.problems-test/problems-cost-nothing-for-a-clean-program}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-observers-defect-is-a-problem-not-invalid-input"
  :seon.task/plan "Still true (re-read 2026-10-23 for step E, its citations brought current): `my.root/problems` answers `(seon.problems/health @(:conn sys) (seon.root/errors sys))`. ```seon.root/errors``` answers the api's fold (`:seon.error/fold`), or, when the api could not answer, its defect (a `:seon.error/base` map: the root task seon.root/a-proc-answers-every-request-it-took); ```seon.problems/health``` and ```seon.problems/report``` take only a fold, so on that defect the problems read itself is refused as invalid input and root sees no problems at all, exactly when something is wrong. Correct: they take the observer's answer as it is: a fold counts as today; a defect is reported as a problem row of its class (an error map is data, AGENTS §4), beside main's own rows, which are counted from main alone. The proof: the report of a db with one recorded error and the defect {:seon.error/type :seon.root/no-answer :seon.error/operation 'seon.root/errors :seon.error/message \"the api did not answer\"} (a whole :seon.error/base map) as the observer's answer holds the recorded error's row and a row naming the defect."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.problems-test/an-observers-defect-is-a-problem-row}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-platform-error-closes-when-its-cause-is-gone"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-25 on the standing root (main): an open :platform row `:seon.watch/died` (its batch handler threw: docs/references.md, No such file or directory), count 1, first and last ms 1792758464909, from an earlier boot. Root's own boot b6569825 runs a watcher that is alive: its `:seon.root/watch` fact has :boot b6569825, 11 batches and no :error. Reproduction (root's REPL): `(let [db @(:conn sys)] {:watch (:seon.root/watch (datahike.api/pull db [:seon.root/watch] [:seon.root/id :root])) :died (->> (my.root/problems sys) :classes :platform :rows (filter #(= :seon.watch/died (:type %))))})` answers a live watch fact and a died row that is not `:open? false`. Cause: `seon.problems/closed?` closes a recorded error only when a later fact is written on its `:seon.error/subject`, a var's name or a section's address. `my.root/watch!`'s `died` records the error by `seon.root/platform-tx` with no subject, so nothing can ever close it: the next boot's watcher writes its fresh `:seon.root/watch` fact, which is the cause being gone, and the reader cannot see it. Correct, at the producer (the existing close rule, no second one): the error names what would show its cause gone as its subject, and `closed?` resolves it as it resolves the others. For the watcher that is root's `:seon.root/watch` fact. The died write lands in the same transaction as the error, so it is not later; only a watcher that started, or a batch it loaded, writes that fact afterwards. That needs `:seon.error/subject` to name an attribute of an entity, e.g. `[[:seon.root/id :root] :seon.root/watch]`, closed when that attribute has a datom after the error's last tx (`d/datoms db :eavt e a`). It is a format change, so it waits for root's gate (§3's four questions). Alternative: the watcher's fact becomes its own entity, `{:seon.root/id :watch}`, so the existing entity subject works with no schema change. Its cost is one more entity, and every root write site of the watch fact moves to it. Recommended: the alternative, since nothing else is written on that entity, so a later datom means a live watcher, and it adds no subject form. The same rule then serves case 4's `:health` task (`seon.reconcile/root-work-is-tasks`), which closes when its row closes. The same holds for a task file refused (notes/stability-2026-10-25.md row 8): seon.problems/closed? closes an error only through :seon.error/subject, and seon.task/file-refused names none, so \"task file my.task.edn: not readable EDN\" stays open after the file reads again. The refused file's error names that file as its subject, which closed? reads."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.watch-test/a-dead-watchers-error-closes-when-a-new-watcher-writes-its-fact}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-section-goes-stale-when-what-it-cites-changes-meaning"
  :seon.task/plan "Waits on the owner's ruling. ```seon.problems/stale-sections``` makes a (section, var) pair stale when the cited var's form digest differs from the one recorded when the section was written, so any edit of a widely cited var stales every section citing it: main shows 223, mostly code churn rather than wrong prose (notes/dogfood/docs-audit.md). Proposed: a section goes stale when a cited var's contract (:malli/schema) or docstring changes, or the var is removed; a body-only change does not. First split main's 223 by kind (absent then, digest changed, no baseline) and count how many each rule keeps."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.problems-test/a-body-change-does-not-stale-a-section seon.problems-test/a-contract-change-stales-a-section}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-problem-row-holds-what-it-knows"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the printer audit (notes/printer-audit-2026-10-25.md; owner, 2026-10-25: \"focus on not generating crap and then not rendering crap\"). A row of ```seon.problems/health``` carries keys that say nothing, required by :seon.problems/row: `:producer nil`, `:at-ms nil`, `:proofs []`, `:answered-by []` on every derived row (```seon.problems/derived-row```, the row fn in ```seon.problems/dead-citations```), and repeats itself: a recorded error's row (```seon.problems/error-row```) holds :example, :signature, :count and :agents both at its top and in :evidence; a dead citation's :evidence repeats its :name and :section. Printed raw (a value that lost its schema's metadata, e.g. (:classes (my.root/problems sys))), the 177 :stale-section rows were 13 keys each, most of them empty. Correct: a row holds a key only when it knows its value (the schema's keys optional, nil meaning unknown never stored), and :evidence only what the row's own keys do not say; its readers (the done and merge gates, seon.render.ai/problem-data, seon.render.ui/problem-row) read a missing key as none. Proof: every row of health on a seeded db has no nil or empty value and no :evidence key equal to its own."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.problems-test/a-problem-row-holds-what-it-knows}}
 {:seon.task/name "closed-names-no-legacy-subject"
  :seon.task/plan "seon.problems/closed? carries a fallback for :seon.docs/refused rows recorded before errors named a subject (the link's target and the citing section's address, read off the example). Every producer names :seon.error/subject now (ff68a7b630, 00d1d3fda3: task files :seon.root/tasks, source reads :seon.root/source; seon.error/asked-tx). Delete the fallback, with a test that every recorded error class names a subject at its producer. Two cases: if runs/store is erased (the owner approved it at the flaky-results merge; it may be skipped now, since that change did not need it), no row without a subject remains and the deletion is all; if it is not erased, the standing store still holds rows without a subject that only the fallback or nothing closes (at least db 1795, task file my.task.edn not readable EDN, and db 1979, git add -A unable to stat notes/root-handoff-2026-10-25.md, both recorded before the subjects), so first retract those signature entities on the owner's go (one write through seon_eval root), then delete the fallback."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "the-health-step-judges-only-what-it-reads"
  :seon.task/plan "`seon.check/branch-health` answered a `:no-<class>` check for every class `:seon.problems/derived` declares, but `seon.problems/caused-split` reads only :dead-citation, :unnamed-site and :stale-section, so :no-stalled, :no-stale-pin, :no-orphan-page, :no-leadless-section, :no-uncited-task and :no-problem-unlinked were true whatever the branch did: six checks that could not fail (AGENTS §9). Corrected at the producer of the verdict: `seon.problems/caused-classes` names the classes `caused-split` reads, and `branch-health` judges those (the default) and refuses any other class asked for, as it already refused an attention class. `seon.check/health-check` reads `seon.problems/health` of head once, not once per class. Deletes the always-true checks; src +4 lines (the def). Wiki: docs/health.md#checks-are-callers-of-seon names the classes."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/a-class-the-health-step-does-not-read-is-refused
                      seon.check-test/the-health-steps-checks-are-the-classes-caused-reads}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-cancelled-tasks-run-problems-close"
  :seon.task/plan "A run's problem rows (`seon.problems/run-rows`) were `:open? (not (task/done? db te))`, and a task root cancelled (`:seon.task/execution :cancelled`) is never done, so its rows stayed open forever (notes/root-repair-findings-2026-10-27.md G8). Corrected at the row's producer: a run's problem is open while its task is still to be worked, not done and not cancelled (the task's execution rides the run's one pull). The :unrun row stays open until its tests pass. src +1 line. Wiki: docs/health.md says a cancelled task's run problems close."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.problems-test/a-cancelled-tasks-run-problems-close}}
 {:seon.task/name "health-reads-each-part-once"
  :seon.task/plan "`seon.problems/health` computed `answers` and `report` computed it again, and called `problem-unlinked` twice and then `distinct`ed the second pass's repeats of the first: a dedupe of its own double work. Corrected: `report` and `health` share `report-with`, which takes the answers read once; `problem-unlinked` judges its own class's page in its one pass, so the second call and the `distinct` go. Contracted calls of one `health` read on the proof's db: 59 before, 51 after (report-with's own contract is one of them); the rows are unchanged (seon.problems-test/an-issue-with-no-page-is-a-problem and problem-unlinked-follows-the-classes-not-their-rows stay green)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.problems-test/health-reads-each-part-once}}
 {:seon.task/name "health-at-the-cost-of-what-changed"
  :seon.task/plan "`seon.render.proc/main-info` read `seon.problems/health` whole each time main moved: the derived classes scanned the wiki and program and `report` read every run of every finished task. Corrected: the proc hands the datoms it already diffs (`d/datoms-diff` of the main it holds and the new one) to `seon.problems/health-moved`, which carries the held rows of the classes `caused-split` reads (`caused-classes`; it now answers `:closed` as well as `:made`, through `split-rows`) and of every run the move did not touch (`touched-runs`), and rereads the rest. The run classes find a touched run without scanning the runs' attributes (`report-with`'s `only`). A run of a finished task is no candidate (`seon.task/done?` is datoms). Measured with a one-run change (`seon.problems-test/problems-move-at-the-cost-of-what-changed`): 34 contracted calls at 10, 100 and 1,000 finished tasks, about 1,000 ticks per task (uncited-tasks' scan); `report` was linear in runs. The two accepted :overrun entries (problems-reads-only-the-problems, problems-cost-nothing-for-a-clean-program) are gone: the reads fit their estimates (the clean program's fixed bytes stated). Replaces `health` of the whole on a move; `section-state` compares what closes a stale row."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.problems-test/problems-move-at-the-cost-of-what-changed seon.problems-test/health-moved-is-health}}]
